# CITATION.cff — https://citation-file-format.github.io/
#
# `doi` is deliberately absent: no DOI has been minted for this software, and a citation
# file that points at an identifier nobody has issued is worse than one that admits it is
# pending. `repository-code` was written when no remote existed and now names the live
# repository.
#
# The fields that state a fact about the repository are the ones a release can move, and
# two of them have gone stale before: `version` (seven spellings, one owner since Phase 30)
# and the abstract's count of numbered limitations, which read `55` for four releases while
# `docs/limitations.md` grew past a hundred because no guard read this file. Both are
# policed now -- `test_documents_that_count_the_limitations_agree_with_the_file` and
# `test_the_version_string_the_project_declares_is_one_number_not_four` -- so a future
# release that forgets a spelling fails a gate instead of publishing a wrong number.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use QuantRisk++ in your research, please cite the software and the technical
  report. Every numerical claim in the report traces to an artifact hashed in
  evidence/manifest.json.
title: "QuantRisk++: A Reproducible C++/Python Engine for Stochastic Pricing, Monte Carlo Simulation, Portfolio Risk, Optimization and Stress Testing"
abstract: >-
  A from-scratch C++20 numerical core with Python facades, covering Black-Scholes-Merton
  pricing, analytic and finite-difference Greeks, a Cox-Ross-Rubinstein lattice, Monte Carlo
  simulation with antithetic and control-variate variance reduction, Asian and barrier
  payoffs, Heston stochastic volatility, historical/Gaussian/Monte Carlo VaR and expected
  shortfall with bootstrap intervals, Kupiec and Christoffersen coverage backtests, three
  covariance estimators, six portfolio optimisers, and a scenario and stress layer with
  Euler attribution. Validation is the product: each component is checked against a closed
  form, a live independent oracle, or a statistical property measured over many
  replications, and 107 numbered limitations record where the claims stop.
authors:
  - name: Zhiren Xiao
    author-affiliation:
      name: School of Applied Statistics, Guangdong University of Finance
repository-code: https://github.com/Jackxiaozhiren/quantrisk-plus-plus
version: 1.9.0
date-released: 2026-10-20
license: MIT
type: software
keywords:
  - quantitative-finance
  - monte-carlo
  - value-at-risk
  - expected-shortfall
  - portfolio-optimization
  - stress-testing
  - reproducibility
  - cxx20
references:
  - type: software
    title: >-
      Design and Validation of a Reproducible C++/Python Stochastic Risk Engine
    authors:
      - name: Zhiren Xiao
    database: repository
    repository-code: https://github.com/Jackxiaozhiren/quantrisk-plus-plus
    release-notes: https://github.com/Jackxiaozhiren/quantrisk-plus-plus/releases/tag/v1.9.0
